<%- include('partials/header', { title: 'Studio Admin FAQ' }) %>

  <main class="container">
    <h1>Studio Admin FAQ & Instructions</h1>

    <section class="faq-section">
      <h2>1. Getting Started: Claiming Your Studio</h2>
      <p>To claim your studio profile and unlock admin features, search for your studio name on the homepage. Click on
        your studio, and then click the "Claim Studio" button next to the title. Once approved by our team, you will
        receive full access to the Management Dashboard.</p>
    </section>


    <section class="faq-section">
      <h2>2. Customizing Your Profile</h2>
      <p>From the Management Dashboard, you can update your public profile. This includes:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Uploading a Studio Logo</li>
        <li>Writing a Bio</li>
        <li>Linking your Instagram and TikTok handles</li>
      </ul>
      <p>These details will immediately reflect on your public studio page, helping dancers and parents find your
        official links.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="faq-section">
      <h2>3. Dashboard Statistics Customization</h2>
      <p>You have full control over what data visitors see on your public studio page. In the Management Dashboard,
        scroll down to the "Public Dashboard Visibility" section to toggle the following metric cards on or off:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Total Awards & Events Attended</li>
        <li>1st Place Finishes (All Time & This Year)</li>
        <li>Activity metrics (Past 5 Years & This Year)</li>
      </ul>
      <p>As an admin or verified owner, you will always be able to see all of your statistics. Cards that are hidden
        from the public will display a red <strong>HIDDEN</strong> badge for your eyes only.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="faq-section">
      <h2>4. Managing Your Dancer Roster & Adding Dancers</h2>
      <p>To protect the privacy and data of our users, we do not allow global searching of dancer names. Instead, you
        can add an existing dancer to your roster by using their <strong>Unique ID</strong>.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Ask the dancer (or their parent) for their Unique ID, which they can find on their public profile URL or
          inside their Dancer Dashboard.</li>
        <li>Go to the <strong>Dancer Roster</strong> tab in your Management Dashboard.</li>
        <li>Click <strong>+ Add/Claim Dancer</strong> and enter the Unique ID. This instantly links their existing
          awards to your studio.</li>
        <li>If the dancer does not exist in the system yet, you can use the same popup to instantly create a new profile
          for them!</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="faq-section">
      <h2>5. Embedding the Website Widget</h2>
      <p>Showcase your studio's achievements natively on your own website! Go to the <strong>Widget Builder</strong> in
        your dashboard. You can customize the background and text colors to match your branding, and simply copy-paste
        the generated <code>&lt;iframe&gt;</code> HTML code directly into your website builder (Wix, Squarespace,
        Wordpress, etc.).</p>
    </section>

    <section class="faq-section">
      <h2>6. Adding Awards to Your Profile</h2>
      <p>You have full control over the awards displayed on your public profile. You can add awards directly from the
        <strong>Studio History</strong> tab or by using the Quick-Add feature in your Studio Dashboard.
      </p>
      <h3>Methods for Adding Awards:</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Search & Claim:</strong> Search for an award by dancer or routine name. If the award already exists
          in our database (from a previous competition import), you can click the "Claim" button next to it to add it
          to your roster.</li>
        <li><strong>Manual Entry:</strong> If an award is not yet in our database, you can click the <strong>+ Add
            Award</strong> button. You can manually enter all details including competition, date, awards, and
          placement.
          For efficiency, the system will remember the "Last Competition" you entered, pre-filling those details for
          subsequent entries.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Once added, the award will appear on your public dashboard and can be used in AI-generated summaries!</p>
    </section>



    <section class="faq-section">
      <h2>7. Curating Your Hall of Fame</h2>
      <p>The <strong>Hall of Fame</strong> on your public profile automatically showcases your studio's top achievements (such as 1st place titles, scholarships, and national finals placements). However, you have full control to override this and curate exactly what is shown.</p>
      <p>Go to your <strong>Studio History</strong> -> <strong>Awards</strong> editor. Next to each award, you will see a <strong>HoF</strong> column with a star icon. Click the icon to cycle through the visibility options:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>☆ Auto-Detect:</strong> Let the system decide if this award is prestigious enough for the Hall of Fame.</li>
        <li><strong style="color: #fbbf24;">⭐ Pinned:</strong> Force this award to always display in the Hall of Fame, regardless of placement or title.</li>
        <li><strong style="color: #ef4444;">🚫 Hidden:</strong> Force this award to never display in the Hall of Fame, even if it was a major win.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="faq-section">
      <h2>8. Generating AI Marketing Summaries</h2>
      <p>Need to write a social media post or press release about your competition results but don't have the time? Our
        platform uses an advanced AI Marketing Summary Generator to do it for you instantly!</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Go to the <strong>Studio History</strong> tab in your dashboard.</li>
        <li>Click the <strong>📄 Generate Text Summary</strong> button next to the competition you want to highlight.
        </li>
        <li>Our system will automatically check the boxes for your major podium placements (1st, 2nd, 3rd, Hope, Grand
          Prix). You can manually check or uncheck any "Top 12/24" awards to completely curate your list.</li>
        <li>Choose your desired Tone ("Enthusiastic" for Instagram or "Professional" for Press Releases) and click
          <strong>✨ Generate AI Summary</strong>.
        </li>
        <li>The AI will instantly write a beautifully formatted, inspiring marketing blurb based <em>only</em> on the
          awards you selected. You can edit the text directly in the box if needed!</li>
      </ul>
    </section>



    <section class="faq-section">
      <h2>8b. Adding Dancers to Group Routines</h2>
      <p><strong>Q: My group awards show no dancers — how do I add the cast?</strong></p>
      <p><strong>A:</strong> Most competitions never publish who danced in group routines, so imported group awards
        arrive without dancer names. Open <strong>Group Routine Dancers</strong> in your management sidebar: every
        group routine is listed by year, and you can paste the cast for each one — one name per line or
        comma-separated. Click <strong>Preview</strong> to see exactly what will happen before anything saves:
        names already on your roster get linked (with a checkbox to untick if the routine's dancer is
        actually a different dancer who shares the name), new names create dancer profiles on your
        roster, and if two of your dancers share a name we'll ask which one danced it — each
        candidate shows their recent routines, and you can give each dancer a <strong>private
        tag</strong> (like "Senior Mia") right there or on your Roster page to tell them apart
        in your own words. Tags are only visible in your management tools, never publicly. Confirming applies the cast to
        <strong>every award that routine won that year</strong>, and each dancer's own trophy case updates
        instantly.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="faq-section">
      <h2>9. The "Pseudo-Studio" Edge Case (Multi-Studio Collabs)</h2>
      <p><strong>Q: What happens if two dancers from two DIFFERENT studios compete together in a Pas De Deux or Group
          (e.g., at YAGP)?</strong></p>
      <p><strong>A:</strong> Our system handles this by creating a "Pseudo-Studio" that literally combines both studio
        names (e.g., "Studio A & Studio B"). This keeps the database fast and prevents structural errors.</p>
      <p>Because it is technically a new combined entity, the award won't automatically appear on your main standalone
        dashboard. However, the award <strong>will</strong> perfectly appear on the individual Dancer's profile. If you
        want the award to also show up on your standalone Studio Widget, you can manually use the <strong>"Add
          Award"</strong> tool in your dashboard to self-report it onto your roster.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="faq-section">
      <h2>10. How does the "Featured Studios" section work?</h2>
      <p><strong>Q: How can my studio get featured on the AwardHome Dance homepage?</strong></p>
      <p><strong>A:</strong> Featured studios are selected automatically — no payment, no favoritism. To be eligible,
        a studio must be <strong>claimed</strong> and have a <strong>logo and bio</strong> on its profile. Among
        eligible studios, selection is based on recent, genuine engagement with your trophy case — things like adding
        new competition results and keeping your roster verified. Recent activity counts more than old activity.</p>
      <p>Two things worth knowing: repeating the same action over and over does <strong>not</strong> multiply your
        standing — the system rewards real upkeep, not volume. And slots rotate: a studio stays featured for a while,
        then rests so others get the spotlight. In short — genuinely maintain your studio's presence, and you'll get
        seen.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="faq-section">
      <h2>11. Dancer Card Photos &amp; Thank-You Notes</h2>
      <p><strong>Q: Can I add photos and thank-you notes to my dancers' award cards?</strong></p>
      <p><strong>A:</strong> Yes. Award cards can flip into a mini-book with a photo page and a thank-you page. As a
        studio director you can manage these for any dancer on your active roster: open <strong>Roster</strong> and
        click the <strong>Card</strong> button on a dancer's row (dancers and parents can also do it themselves from
        their Dancer Dashboard).</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Photos:</strong> each award can carry its own photo (usually the routine's performance shot),
          plus one default card photo per dancer as the fallback. Your first upload for a dancer asks for a
          one-time consent confirmation covering everyone pictured; later uploads skip it.</li>
        <li><strong>Thank-you notes:</strong> a short line per award (280 characters). On group routines every dancer
          gets their own line — teachers are usually the first ones thanked, so encourage your dancers to fill these
          in!</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Everything is reviewed by the AwardHome team before it appears publicly, and edits go back into review.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="faq-section">
      <h2>12. Confirming Dancer Profile Claims</h2>
      <p><strong>Q: Why do dancer profile claims appear in my Verifications dashboard?</strong></p>
      <p><strong>A:</strong> When a parent or dancer claims a dancer profile using your <strong>Studio Claim Code</strong>,
        the claim is routed to you instead of the general review queue — you know your families, so your confirmation
        is the fastest and most reliable check. You'll get an email when one arrives; open
        <strong>Pending Verifications → Profile Claims to Confirm</strong> and Confirm or Deny.</p>
      <p>The code only proves they're part of your studio community — please confirm the person actually belongs to
        <em>that dancer's</em> family before approving, since approval hands them full control of the profile. The
        claimant is emailed the decision automatically. Claims without a code (or with a wrong code) go to the
        AwardHome team instead.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="faq-section">
      <h2>13. Planning Next Season: the Upcoming Events Directory</h2>
      <p><strong>Q: Is there a way to see which competitions are coming up near us?</strong></p>
      <p><strong>A:</strong> Yes — the <a href="/dance/events">Upcoming Events directory</a> gathers published tour
        dates from competitions across the platform in one place. Filter by <strong>state</strong>,
        <strong>month</strong>, or <strong>competition</strong> to map out your season without building the
        spreadsheet yourself, and follow each listing's registration link when you're ready to enter. Dates come from
        organizers' published schedules; always confirm dates and venues with the organizer before booking travel.</p>
    </section>
  </main>

